{"type":"tool_execution_update","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01TAYUVkWgpR2oMWdWSm2py4","toolName":"subagent","args":{"agent":"refactor","agentScope":"both","task":"Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: 10 (all tests in the test list are now implemented and green)\nRecent changes: Added tests for single-cell death, block still-life, blinker oscillation (both transitions), and negative-coordinate handling. All of these passed without implementation changes because the existing survive/reproduce/candidate-set algorithm already generalizes correctly.\n\nThis is the final refactor pass for the whole feature (Conway's Game of Life next-generation function). Do a full review pass over the implementation file for clarity, naming, duplication, and unnecessary complexity, given that all 10 spec-derived tests are now passing.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."},"partialResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let's evaluate the rest of the file more carefully for naming, duplication, and complexity.\n\n**Naming Evaluation (First Priority)**\n\n- `key(x, y)` — generic name; in context of a Game of Life grid, `cellKey` better reveals intent (a string identity for a cell).\n- `neighborsOf(x, y)` — clear, fits its purpose.\n- `countLiveNeighbors` — clear and accurate.\n- `survives` / `reproduces` — accurately named per Conway's rules; fits.\n- Inside `nextGeneration`, the ternary `isAlive ? survives(neighbors) : reproduces(neighbors)` computes \"will this candidate be alive next generation\" — that composite concept has no name currently. Extracting it as `willBeAlive(isAlive, neighborCount)` would make the loop body read as a sentence.\n- `live` (the Set) — acceptable but `liveCells` is more precise given `cells` is the domain type name.\n- Candidates are stored as `Set<string>` and then decoded back into `[x, y]` via `.split(\",\").map(Number)` — this is duplication of the coordinate information already known when the candidate was added, and it's an extra parsing step (string ⇄ number round-trip) that obscures intent and adds unnecessary invocations.\n\nDecision: Rename `key` → `cellKey`, `live` → `liveCells`, `result` → `nextGenerationCells`; extract `willBeAlive`; store candidates as `Map<string, Cell>` to avoid the string-parsing round-trip."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_06-24-01_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_sonnet-5/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: 10 (all tests in the test list are now implemented and green)\nRecent changes: Added tests for single-cell death, block still-life, blinker oscillation (both transitions), and negative-coordinate handling. All of these passed without implementation changes because the existing survive/reproduce/candidate-set algorithm already generalizes correctly.\n\nThis is the final refactor pass for the whole feature (Conway's Game of Life next-generation function). Do a full review pass over the implementation file for clarity, naming, duplication, and unnecessary complexity, given that all 10 spec-derived tests are now passing.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: 10 (all tests in the test list are now implemented and green)\nRecent changes: Added tests for single-cell death, block still-life, blinker oscillation (both transitions), and negative-coordinate handling. All of these passed without implementation changes because the existing survive/reproduce/candidate-set algorithm already generalizes