ged.\n\n**Rule 3 — No Duplication**\n- The `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS` IIFE (nested loop generating 8 static offsets) was pure duplication of a static table computed at every module load; replaced with a literal array — same value, no runtime loop, and immediately readable as \"the 8 Moore-neighborhood directions.\"\n- The candidate set previously stored cells as encoded `\"x,y\"` strings, then decoded them back via `.split(\",\").map(Number)` inside the loop — this was a round-trip duplication of information already known at insertion time. Replaced `Set<string>` with `Map<string, Cell>` (`collectCandidates`) so the original `Cell` tuple is preserved and never needs re-parsing.\n\n**Rule 4 — Fewest Elements**\n- Extracted `collectCandidates` as a named step, separating \"which cells might change\" from \"did each one survive/reproduce\" inside `nextGeneration`, making the main function a short, linear sequence of named steps.\n\n### APP Mass — Before / After\n\n**Before (full file `nextGeneration` + inline candidate-building + string-parsing loop):**\n- `neighborsOf`: bindings 3, invocations 2(map)+... ≈ 5 invocations-ish — kept unchanged, mass roughly same both versions, so focusing delta on `nextGeneration` body:\n- Constants: `0` params etc ≈ 2\n- Bindings: `live, candidates, x, y, nx, ny, result, candidateKey, neighbors, isAlive` ≈ 10 → 10\n- Invocations: `Set()`, `.map`, `key()`×2, `.add`×2 (in loop), `.split`, `.map(Number)`, `countLiveNeighbors()`, `.has`, `survives()`/`reproduces()` (ternary counts as 1 invocation branch), `.push` ≈ 12 → mass 24\n- Conditionals: ternary ×1 = 4\n- Loops: `for` outer, `for` inner (neighbors), `for` over candidates = 3 → mass 15\n- Assignments: 0 (all `const`)\n\nTotal (nextGeneration body only) ≈ 2 + 10 + 24 + 4 + 15 = **55**\n\n**After (`nextGeneration` + `collectCandidates` + `willBeAlive`, same responsibilities):**\n- Constants: 0 (none new) ≈ 0\n- Bindings: `liveCells, candidates, nextGenerationCells, candidateKey, cell, x, y, liveNeighborCount, isAlive` (nextGeneration) + `candidates, x, y, neighbor` (collectCandidates) + `isAlive, liveNeighborCount` (willBeAlive) ≈ 15\n- Invocations: `Set()`, `.map`, `cellKey()`, `collectCandidates()`, `countLiveNeighbors()`, `.has`, `willBeAlive()`, `.push` (nextGeneration: 8) + `cellKey()`×2, `neighborsOf()`, `.set`×2 (collectCandidates: 5) + `survives()`, `reproduces()` (willBeAlive: 2) ≈ 15 → mass 30\n- Conditionals: 1 ternary (in `willBeAlive`) + 1 `if` (in `nextGeneration`) = 2 → mass 8\n- Loops: `for` in `nextGeneration`, `for` outer + `for` inner in `collectCandidates` = 3 → mass 15\n- Assignments: 0\n\nTotal (equivalent responsibilities, now spread across 3 named functions) ≈ 0 + 15 + 30 + 8 + 15 = **68**\n\n**Mass Change: 55 → 68 (Δ +13)**\n\nThis refactor slightly *increases* raw APP mass because the string-parsing round-trip was replaced with an extra named function and a `Map`, and the ternary was extracted into its own function with its own parameter bindings. Per the guidelines, **Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) trumps APP** here: the mass increase buys a codebase where `nextGeneration` reads as a named, top-to-bottom sequence (`collectCandidates` → `countLiveNeighbors` → `willBeAlive`) with no manual string decoding, which is a meaningful clarity and correctness-safety win (eliminating the decode step also removes a latent class of bugs if the key format ever changed).\n\n**Tests**: All 10 tests still pass; `tsc --noEmit` is clean.\n\n---\n\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: Renamed `key`→`cellKey`, `live`→`liveCells`, `result`→`nextGenerationCells`; replaced the neighbor-offset IIFE with a static literal table; extracted `willBeAlive` to name the survive/reproduce decision; extracted `collectCandidates` and switched candidate storage from `Set<string>` (with string-parsing decode) to `Map<string, Cell>` to eliminate duplicated coordinate encoding/decoding.\n**Mass Change**: 55 -> 68 (+13, accepted per Rule 2 — clarity over raw mass)\n**Tests